  "discover": {
    "intro": "Spanish has two verbs that both mean \"to be\". Before I explain anything, can you crack the code?",
    "groups": [
      {
        "label": "With ser (soy, es, somos)",
        "tone": "blue",
        "lines": [
          [
            "Soy de Inglaterra.",
            "I'm from England."
          ],
          [
            "Mi hermana es médica.",
            "My sister is a doctor."
          ],
          [
            "Somos amigos.",
            "We are friends."
          ],
          [
            "La paella es deliciosa.",
            "Paella is delicious."
          ]
        ]
      },
      {
        "label": "With estar (estoy, está, estamos)",
        "tone": "orange",
        "lines": [
          [
            "Estoy en Madrid.",
            "I'm in Madrid."
          ],
          [
            "Estoy cansada.",
            "I'm tired."
          ],
          [
            "Estamos viajando.",
            "We're travelling."
          ],
          [
            "La sopa está fría.",
            "The soup is cold."
          ]
        ]
      }
    ],
    "question": "What do the blue sentences have in common, compared with the orange ones?",
    "options": [
      "Blue = what someone or something IS (identity, origin, job). Orange = where it is, or how it is right now.",
      "Blue = past tense. Orange = present tense.",
      "Blue = formal Spanish. Orange = informal Spanish."
    ],
    "answer": 0,
    "nudge": "Not quite. Look at the verbs in each colour again (soy, es, somos against estoy, estamos, está), then try another.",
