  "learn": {
    "title": "The verbs",
    "sub": "Tap any row to hear it",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "type": "list",
        "title": "Who's who: the people in a Spanish verb",
        "rows": [
          ["yo", "I"],
          ["tú", "you: one person you know (friends, family, people your age)"],
          ["usted", "you: one person, polite (a stranger, someone older, a customer)"],
          ["él / ella", "he / she"],
          ["nosotros / nosotras", "we (nosotras when everyone is female)"],
          ["vosotros / vosotras", "you all, informal: everyday in Spain"],
          ["ustedes", "you all, polite"],
          ["ellos / ellas", "they (ellas when everyone is female)"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "tip",
        "text": "Spanish has four ways to say \"you\": tú and usted for one person, vosotros and ustedes for several. Usted takes the same verb form as él and ella (usted es, ella es), and ustedes the same as ellos (ustedes son). Each person has its own verb ending, so the pronoun is usually left out: soy already means \"I am\"."
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "title": "Four ways to say you",
        "rows": [
          ["tú", "you: one person you know well (a friend, family, a child)"],
          ["usted", "you: one person, polite (a stranger, someone older, at work)"],
          ["vosotros / vosotras", "you all: several people you know well (used all the time in Spain)"],
          ["ustedes", "you all: several people, polite"]
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "tables",
        "tables": [
          {
            "title": "ser",
            "tone": "blue",
            "rows": [
              [
                "yo",
                "soy"
              ],
              [
                "tú",
                "eres"
              ],
              [
                "él / ella / usted",
                "es"
              ],
              [
                "nosotros/as",
                "somos"
              ],
              [
                "vosotros/as",
                "sois"
              ],
              [
                "ellos / ellas / ustedes",
                "son"
              ]
            ]
          },
          {
            "title": "estar",
            "tone": "orange",
            "rows": [
              [
                "yo",
                "estoy"
              ],
              [
                "tú",
                "estás"
              ],
              [
                "él / ella / usted",
                "está"
              ],
              [
                "nosotros/as",
                "estamos"
              ],
              [
                "vosotros/as",
                "estáis"
              ],
              [
                "ellos / ellas / ustedes",
                "están"
              ]
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "spain",
        "text": "In Spain you will hear vosotros (informal \"you all\") constantly, so it is in the tables from day one."
      },
      {
        "type": "twist",
        "title": "Same word, different meaning",
        "a": [
          "La película es aburrida.",
          "The film is boring."
        ],
        "b": [
          "Estoy aburrid{o}.",
          "I'm bored."
        ],
        "note": "With ser you describe what something is like. With estar you describe how it is or how you feel right now. (Handy: you can now say whether something is boring, or whether you are!)"
      },
      {
        "type": "note",
        "kind": "warn",
        "text": "Sleepy, hungry, thirsty and age use TENER, not ser or estar. Tengo sueño. Tengo hambre. Tengo treinta años."
      }
    ]
  },
